    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> seceded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> on 28 September 1961; the Ba'athist coup of 8 March 1963 then founded a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> that ruled under martial law until 2011 and fell in December 2024.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafez-al-assad">Hafez al-Assad</a>'s Corrective Movement of November 1970 made the Ba'athist state a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>: Alawite loyalists held the key posts, a cult of personality surrounded the family, and his son Bashar succeeded him in 2000.</li><li>The uprising that began on 26 January 2011 became a thirteen-year civil war with more than 600,000 dead, over 5 million refugees and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> that shrank by 84% between 2010 and 2023; Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 8 December 2024.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>By 2021 the amphetamine captagon, produced by associates and relatives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashar-al-assad">Bashar al-Assad</a>, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s largest export at a minimum of US$3.4 billion a year, against about US$122 million for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, its largest legal export.</p></aside>
    </section>

